l loading. For clarity, you might want to skip rendering `collections` entirely when `loading` is true.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/frontend-manage/src/components/resources/AnswerCollectionItem.tsx (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`25-44`: **Consider externalizing or reusing the access map.**  \nIf multiple components will rely on mapping `CollectionAccess` to UI representations, refactor this map into a shared utility to avoid duplicating logic.\n\n---\n\n`46-85`: **Potential UX improvement for read-only mode.**  \nWhen `editable` is false, consider clarifying the user flow for viewing shared content, possibly using a tooltip or additional text for consistency with the displayed “VIEW SHARED CONTENT” button text.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/frontend-manage/src/components/resources/AnswerCollections.tsx (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-33`: **Handle loading and error states for better user experience.**  \nCurrently, the UI simply destructures `data` and `loading` from the query without handling errors or providing a fallback loader/spinner. Consider explicitly handling `error` using `useQuery` or showing a loading indicator to enhance user feedback.  \n\n```diff\n function AnswerCollections() {\n   const t = useTranslations()\n-  const { data, loading } = useQuery(GetAnswerCollectionsDocument)\n+  const { data, loading, error } = useQuery(GetAnswerCollectionsDocument)\n\n   if (loading) {\n+    return <div>Loading collections...</div>\n   }\n\n+  if (error) {\n+    return <div>Error loading collections: {error.message}</div>\n+  }\n```\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>packages/graphql/src/services/resources.ts (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`111-113`: **Consider clarifying null returns for missing users.**  \nIn GraphQL, returning `null` for unauthorized or non-existent users is common, but a more specific error can help the client distinguish between an actual permission problem and a missing user. If this 